<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n24789" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">in-vīsus</span></b> , a, um, adj.
		<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Unseen</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sacra maribus non invisa solum, sed etiam
					inaudita,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Har. Resp. 27, 57</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">morbus,</span>”
				<i>unknown</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Cato, R. R. 141, 2</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">invisus atque inaccessus in id tempus saltus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Flor. 4, 12, 27</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Adv.</i>: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">invīsē</span></b> , <i>without
				being seen</i> (post-class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">invise advenerat,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Fulg. Myth. 3, 6</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
